    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "52355c54_4e1d3157",
        "filename": "system/gd/rust/linux/mgmt/src/state_machine.rs",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1432,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-31T05:17:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I noticed that we usually get `BluetoothStarted` and `BluetoothStopped` after this because the original `TurningOn` eventually completed. The events are:\n1. pid file created (caused by original `TurningOn`)\n2. pid file removed (caused by the `stop` call here)\n3. pid file created (caused by the `start` call here)\n\nThe current state machine implementation we\u0027ll do:\n1. pid file created \u003d Got `BluetoothStarted` on `TurningOn` -\u003e Turned `On` successfully\n2. pid file removed \u003d Got `BluetoothStopped` on `On` -\u003e Unexpectedly turned `Off`, try restarted (but we already called `start` so it\u0027s no-op)\n3. pid file created \u003d Got `BluetoothStarted` on `TurningOn` -\u003e Turned `On` successfully\n\nSo eventually we still restarted and got `On`, but the log would be confusing. I\u0027m thinking whether we should:\n1. Don\u0027t do anything here and change to a new state `WaitingRestarting`\n2. In `action_on_bluetooth_started`, if the previous state is `WaitingRestarting`, call `stop` and change to `Restarting`\n\n@yinghsu@google.com and @jiangzp@google.com, WDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "8c69a913_34ae13eb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1428,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 1432,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "05bb0734dc268627a38359c348b870d3106f511e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
